course: commands
lesson: 1
title: Imperatives and polite requests
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A command tells someone to do something: "pass the salt", "shut the door". It usually starts with the action word and leaves out "you", because "you" is whoever is being told.
explain: Polite requests wrap the same command in softer words: "could you…", "would you mind…", "I'd like you to…", "be a love and…". They are still commands, and the real answer is doing the job, not saying "yes, I could".
explain: Real people also chop commands down to a word or two, like "oi. remote. now.", and the scene fills in the missing action. People who take only the exact words, like a robot, get it wrong.
explain: A good listener works out what is wanted, who must do it and when, then does it and says "done". If you are holding something hot or sharp, or you are mid-game, you make it safe or pause first, then go.
explain: "Please" makes a request kinder, but it is not what makes it a command. A sharp "PLEASE" after the third time means "now, and I'm getting cross".
fact: A command with your name on it, or "mate" or "love" said to you, is a job for you, usually right now.
fact: "Someone, quick" means the person best placed to do it, usually the one nearest.
fact: Words with no action word, like "remote, now", can still be a command; the scene supplies the action.
fact: In much of Britain "fetch us" or "give us" means "fetch me" or "give me".
fact: "Love", "mate", "pet" and "my 'andsome" (Cornwall) are friendly words, not jokes or insults; in Cornwall "maid" means a girl and "lush" means lovely.
fact: "Could you…?" or "can you…?" with the job right in front of you is a polite command; you answer by doing it.
fact: When you will do it, "would you mind…?" is answered "no, not at all", because "yes" means "yes, I do mind".
fact: "I'd like you to…" from a teacher or parent is an order said kindly, not a wish you may ignore.
fact: "Be a love and…" is a friendly command, and "put the kettle on" means boil water for tea.
fact: A command often gives an action and a place, like "sit ON the benches", and both parts count.
fact: "Help me" asks for a hand; "help yourself" means you may take some.
fact: In the UK the "boot" of a car is the US "trunk"; "chuck" means throw; in Australia "togs" are a swimming costume.
fact: In Ireland "would you ever…" is a polite way to ask; "rn" means right now, "lowkey" means a bit, and "no cap" means honestly.
fact: People do interrupt when it matters, like a pan boiling over; you make your own job safe first, then help.
exercise: Dad, at the dinner table: "pass the salt, mate." Leo is nine. What does Dad want? | *Leo to pick up the salt and hand it to him now | Leo to tell him where on the table the salt is | Leo to pass it along after everyone has eaten | Leo to point at the salt so Dad can reach it
exercise: Leo just points and says "it's there". What happens next? | *Dad stretches over the gravy and knocks it over | Dad says thanks, pointing is just as good as passing | Dad is pleased that Leo did what was asked | Nothing, pointing is the same as passing
exercise: Teacher at the door, worksheets flying: "shut the door, someone, quick". You sit at the back by the window. What do you do? | Jump up and race the whole class to the door | *Stay sat and let the kid nearest the door shut it | Put your hand up and ask who she means by someone | Shout "someone's on it" and carry on with work
exercise: In that classroom, who should shut the door? | Every child in the class all at once | *The child sitting nearest the door | The teacher, because she said it | The last child who came in this morning
exercise: Big sister flat on the sofa: "oi. remote. now." What is she asking for? | Nothing, because it isn't a proper sentence | *The remote, handed to her straight away | Where the remote is, so she can get it | The telly turned off with the remote
exercise: Nan in a Yorkshire kitchen: "fetch us the big pan, love, it's in t' bottom cupboard." Who is "us" here? | The whole family | Nan and her grandson | *Just Nan herself | Everyone in the street
exercise: What does her grandson do? | Fetches the whole family into the kitchen | Laughs, because "love" shows that she is only joking | Looks in the top cupboard for a small pan | *Gets the big pan from the bottom cupboard for Nan
exercise: PE teacher in the hall: "Year 5, sit on the benches. On them, not next to them." Where does a Year 5 child sit? | On the floor beside a bench | *On a bench | On the floor facing the benches | Wherever they sat last week
exercise: Mum in the supermarket car park: "could you grab the bags from the boot, please?" What is the best reply? | "Yeah, I could" and stay in your seat | "Yes" and wait for her to say "now" | *"Sure" and carry the bags in from the boot | "Which bags?" though only shopping is there
exercise: Ava answered "yeah, I could" and stayed in the car. What happened? | Mum was pleased with such an honest answer | Mum asked again more nicely and then waited | *Mum carried five bags alone and dropped the eggs | Nothing; it was only a question about Ava
exercise: The neighbour, over the fence: "would you mind chucking the ball back, mate?" You're happy to. What do you say? | "Yes, I mind" and throw it back | *"No, course not" and throw it back | "Yes, I would" and throw it back | "Yeah, I'd mind" and keep the ball
exercise: What does "yes" to "would you mind…?" really say? | That you're happy to do it right away | That you didn't quite hear the question | That you'll do it after you've had tea | *That you do mind and would rather not
exercise: Teacher, calm voice: "Marcus, I'd like you to put all four legs of that chair on the floor." What must Marcus do? | Nothing, because "I'd like" is only a wish of hers | Stand up and push the chair under the desk | Keep rocking, but a bit more quietly | *Stop rocking and sit with the chair flat now
exercise: Grandad in Cornwall, to his granddaughter, who may use the kettle: "be a lush maid and put the kettle on, my 'andsome." What does he want? | The kettle put on top of the cupboard | A maid fetched in to make the tea for him | *The kettle filled and switched on for his tea | The kettle carried over and handed to him
exercise: Her little cousin is not allowed near hot water yet, and Grandad asks him the same. What is the best move? | Boil it anyway, since Grandad asked him so nicely | Pretend not to hear and keep on playing | Put the cold kettle on the table for him | *Tell Grandad and ask him to do the hot part
exercise: Mum by the front door, after asking twice: "Leo. Shoes. PLEASE." What does that sharp "please" mean? | He can choose, because she said please | She's being extra polite, so no rush | *Shoes on right now; she's getting cross | She wants Leo to say please back to her
exercise: Dad under the car bonnet: "help me out here, pass the torch." Later Mum at the party: "help yourselves to crisps." What do the two "help"s mean? | Both mean sort yourselves out with no help | *Dad wants a hand; Mum says you may take some | Dad wants the car mended by you alone | Mum wants you to serve all the guests first
exercise: You're carrying a full mug of hot cocoa when Nan says "fetch us the big pan, love". What do you do first? | Drop the mug and run to the cupboard | Fetch the pan with the mug still in your hand | Say you never stop a job halfway through | *Put the mug down safely, then fetch the pan
test: Your mate at the chippy: "can u pass the ketchup". What's the right answer? | Say "yeah, I can" | Say "my arms work fine, ta" | *Pass him the ketchup | Point to where it is
test: Teacher by the board: "Right. Pencils. Down." What does she want? | Someone to pick a pencil up off the floor | All the pencils passed down to the back | Nothing; it isn't a full sentence | *Every pencil put down on the desk now
test: A friend says, "if they don't say please it doesn't count as an order, innit." Is he right? | Yes, without please you can just ignore it | Yes, please is what makes it a real order | No, but only adults' orders count at all | *No, an order counts with or without please
test: Your Gen Z cousin: "bestie, lowkey need you to grab my charger rn, no cap." What is she asking? | Charge your own phone later | Say if you've seen a charger | *Bring her the charger now | Buy her a new one tomorrow
test: An Aussie mum: "be a sweetheart and chuck your togs on the line, they're dripping." What does she want? | Swimming costume thrown at the line for fun | Swimming costume put back on to dry it off | Dripping towels thrown out in the wheelie bin | *Wet swimming costume hung on the washing line
test: Gran: "can you give us a hand with these bags?" Your little brother holds out his hand to her. What did he get wrong? | Nothing; he did exactly what she said | He should have held out both his hands | *She wanted help carrying, not his hand | She only wanted him to wave hello
test: You're halfway through a level when Dad shouts, "Mia, come and hold the ladder, NOW." What is the sensible order? | Finish the level, then go to Dad | Say "never interrupt me!" and play on | Ignore it, as he didn't say please | *Pause the game, then go to Dad
test: Mum is telling a story when Dad cuts in: "sorry, love, pan's boiling over, someone turn it down." Was Dad rude? | *No, a pan boiling over is a good reason to cut in | Yes, you must never interrupt anybody at all | Yes, he should have waited till the story ended | No, but only because he remembered to say "love"
test: Auntie in Cork: "ah, would you ever stick the kettle on, pet?" What does she want? | *The kettle put on to boil, asked nicely | To know if you have ever boiled a kettle | A sticker stuck on the side of the kettle | You to say "never", as you haven't yet
test: Teacher: "Year 5, line up at the door. Year 6, stay in your seats." You're in Year 6. What do you do? | Line up at the door | Line up behind Year 5 | Stand by your chair | *Stay in your seat
test: You fetched the bags Mum asked for, but one bag split and the tins rolled under the car. What do you say? | Nothing at all; she'll spot the tins by herself later | "Done!" and quietly push the split bag in the bin | *"Done, but one bag split and tins are under the car" | "I couldn't do it", even though you nearly did it all
exam: It is given that a teacher states, "I'd like you to put your chair down". Identify the nature of the utterance. | A statement of private preference, freely disregarded | An enquiry concerning the pupil's own wishes in the matter | A remark addressed to no person in particular | *A directive in courteous terms, requiring compliance
exam: Determine the reply appropriate to the enquiry "Would you mind returning the ball?" on the part of a listener who intends to comply. | "Yes", and the ball returned forthwith | *"No, not at all", and the ball returned | "Yes", and the ball retained | No reply, the enquiry being rhetorical
exam: Where the instruction "Shut the door, someone" is issued to an assembled class, identify the party upon whom the obligation to act is thereby imposed. | Every pupil present, all of them acting simultaneously | No pupil, no name having been specified | *The pupil most favourably positioned, being nearest | The speaker herself, having raised the matter
exam: Ascertain the significance of the word "please" when uttered sharply after a request has twice been repeated without effect. | That compliance has thereby become optional | That thanks are given for compliance received | That the time of compliance may be negotiated | *That immediate compliance is now required
